A||||17||BC-GT||roof racks? to carry a 18 foot kayak?||||1||
Z||000000||BC-GT||04-14-2001||10:14 AM||||My sister really wants a Fiero now thats she has seen mine but she carries around a 18 foot kayak sometimes. Is there any roof racks that fit? Car seems kinda small to me for that(the roof anyways)<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||04-14-2001||11:53 AM||||Well, I'm just guessing here, but I don't think it would work out.  Even if you found a roof rack that would fit, there would be too much extra hanging over the edges.  The roof's gotta be, what, 4 feet deep?  That's 7 feet unsupported on each side, front and back.  It might actually damage the roof or the kayak.  I'm not sure though.||||reg||12
Z||000002||sjp777||04-14-2001||11:56 AM||||You're better off finding a rack for the kayak to carry the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||6
Z||000003||GoCartGuy||04-14-2001||02:55 PM||||You're in luck I know of one company that makes racks to fit Fiero's.  The name is Thule, its a Sweedish company.  They make a specific item for small cars called a short roof adapter.  Here's a link to their page. <A HREF="http://www.thuleracks.com/thule/fg_vehicle.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thuleracks.com/thule/fg_vehicle.asp</A> <P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-14-2001||03:21 PM||||GoCart, don't encourage them! Do you know how ridiculous that Fiero would look with an 18 foot kayak???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Although I would have to agree with fropuff. Which will crack first the roof or the kayak?<P>My money would be on the kayak on the first street dip or speed bump you have to go through.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-14-2001||03:38 PM||||just the thought of that..... 18 feet... The thing has to be as long at the car or close to it. I don't think any rack on that small of a roof would be safe. even if you tied it at the bow and stern... the rocking would likely damage panels on the car.<P>At the very least you'd have to use a roof rack with a frame to fit between the back of the deck lid and the kayak. even then I'd be worried about it.<P>Only safe thing I can think of is a custom modified trailer. something that would carry the boat on an angle so you could get the bow really close or even over the deck lid some to keep the trailer short.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||04-14-2001||06:08 PM||||If it's Fibreglass, then that 18ft kayak probably won't weigh more than 40-50lbs. I think that the roof should easily be able to support it, with that rack on the Thule site.  I'm planning on attaching a Thule sarcophagus to the top of mine... as long as it's tied down securely enough to prevent any movement, there shouldn't be a problem. That Fiero roof is very strong. Bypass the enduroflex with a rack. Shouldn't have a problem<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000007||GoCartGuy||04-14-2001||06:12 PM||||Check the website for a garauntee, if I remember right it was no damage to what your hauling or the car if its installed properly but you'd have to check it out.  BTW, I've seen a kayak hauled by a VW Rabbit...now that's funny.  And most kayaks are about 70 lbs.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000008||N_Man||04-14-2001||08:06 PM||||Dont do it...<P>I work at a bike shop and ride mountain bikes regularly, we used to sell racks, and our supplier didnt want to sell me a rack because his book said that the fiero roof could only support 42 pounds.  Not enough for 2 bikes and certainly not enough for a 18 foot kayak.<P>My bet is that, best case the roof panel would crack and worst case the panel would crack and yould would loose the rack and the load.<P>Ive learned that mooching rides to the mountains isnt that hard, just need to have riding buddies with trucks and minivans.  The same may work for your sister if she has kayak buddoes||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||04-14-2001||09:35 PM||||Did anyone mention that police will probably not be happy with it being larger than vehicle? At best shes going to have to flag the front and rear of the kayak.Theyd prob give her a ticket for having unsafe load .Id check laws in your state on that. Either way, tell her to get a beater car to carry her boat. Not pertinent here but I once got passed by an old LTD on the freeway with a surplus guided missile tied to the roof. People will do anything, lol.<P>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-14-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||04-14-2001||11:06 PM||||The Thule roof rack and a trailer hitch with one of those extension/support things would probably work pretty well.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BC-GT||04-14-2001||11:10 PM||||Thanks to all for your input, like I intially thought, it's not a good idea. I'll let her know but she ain't gonna be happy <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Hank is Here||04-14-2001||11:58 PM||||I have a roof rack   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <BR>It was in a parts car once, never had a reason to use it and never think I will.  Sort of the most pointless accessory <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||04-15-2001||01:26 AM||||The roof will only support 42 pounds?  Guess I've gotten lucky, I've sat on the roof plenty of times.. hasn't collapsed yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Remember, we pretty much have a full roll-cage supporting the roof because of the space frame.  <P>I work at a bike shop, too, and I'm thinking about pro-forming the Thule...||||reg||1
Z||000014||Black88GT||04-15-2001||01:34 AM||||I'm with Standard, on my old Formula we had about 4 people standing on the roof once.  42 lbs. is a joke.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||N_Man||04-15-2001||03:59 AM||||gotta remember that 42 pounds will be very concentrated at the 4 mounting points and wind loads will add significantly to that...<P>My guess is that when you sit on it the load is probably spread out much better and that there is no wind gusts to concentrate the weight even further.<P>Oh and ive also had roof racks on a few cars and you definitly feel wind gusts MUCH more, so i think the 42 pounds is definitly subjective to circumstance.  BUT is a valid estimate.||||reg||1
Z||000016||bHooper||04-15-2001||12:37 PM||||i used to have a roof rack on my GT, that i carried my mountain bike on.  i can't remeber what brand it was, it was similar to the thule, but different.<P>never had any problems with the roof cracking, but the cheap @$$ clearcoat rubbed off, where the rack "feet" were!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's<BR>and spellcheck crash's windows<BR>Poly Bushings<BR>Accel Coil||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||04-15-2001||01:20 PM||||the problem isn't holding the weight up. You won't cave it in even if you crack the pannel.<P>The problem is keeping the load from tearing off the roof panel. You are seriously lacking for solid anchor points. The 42# number is all they probably thought the anchor points the rack uses would take without tearing thru the panel. The panel is only attched at a few small bolts arround the opening in the fram where the sunroof goes. The center area is unsupported. If you cocentrate a load there it could punch a hole in the panel.||||reg||1
